+\paragraph{Certified issuance and recursive virtual bases.}
+A datatype may give an issuance predicate $G(e,\sigma)$, checked at the
+materialized head where the original event is created. An issuance-certified
+execution retains this original mint evidence and a visibility-respecting
+replay of each event's original causal past at which its guard holds.
+The raw application rule itself has no such guard. Guards are not checked
+again when an already issued effector is replayed in a merge proof.
+
+The recursive virtual-base extension also permits a merge whose heads have
+several incomparable maximal common ancestors. It synthesizes a base for
+their intersection history by recursively merging the maximal common
+ancestors, using recursively computed bases for the inner merges. The nested recursion decreases finite ancestry support, with the pending
+fold length as its inner measure. This
+construction creates no update event and does not mint its scratch versions
+into the store. Its correctness is an additional execution theorem, requiring
+representation preservation for both the inner unions and their intersection
+bases. We state ordinary and recursive-virtual certified guarantees separately;
+neither follows by choosing an arbitrary maximal common ancestor in the
+ordinary rule.
