+from the tombstoned RGA: deletion removes a record, while insertion carries
+an immutable coordinate describing its placement. The correctness statement
+below concerns this concrete implementation and its original issuer.
+
+\paragraph{State and operations.}
+Let $A$ be an inhabited value domain with decidable equality. A record is $(i,x,k)$, where $i\in\mathbb N$ is an
+insertion identifier, $x\in A$, and $k$ is a bit-string coordinate. Fix an
+ordered prefix code $\Gamma$ for positive timestamp differences. Its
+order-preservation and prefix-freeness properties ensure that the coordinates
+of distinct, honestly issued births have distinct placement keys. Concrete
+states are lists of records, initially empty. The query returns their values
+in list order.
+
+An update payload is either $\F{ins}(x,p,a)$, carrying an anchor identifier $a$
+and its coordinate $p$, or $\F{del}(i)$. An insertion event
+$e=(t,r,\F{ins}(x,p,a))$ allocates identifier $t$ and coordinate
+\[
+ k(e)=p\cdot\Gamma(t-a).
+\]
+Its effector inserts $(t,x,k(e))$ in descending placement-key order unless the
+identifier is already present. A deletion filters out the record with the
+named identifier. These effectors are total: replay does not rerun the issuer.
+The query ignores identifiers and coordinates, but implementation equality
+compares the entire record list.
+
+For an ancestor list $l$ and branch lists $a,b$, merge retains a record from
+$a$ when its identifier occurs in $b$ or is absent from $l$. It then retains
+records from $b$ whose identifiers occur in neither $l$ nor $a$. A sorted
+merge of these lists gives the result. On represented inputs, equal identifiers
+have the same immutable record. This is the observed-remove survival rule
+applied to ordered records.
+
+\paragraph{Original issuance and the independent specification.}
+Insertion at a materialized head is permitted when $a<t$ and either
+$a=0,p=[]$, or that head contains a record $(a,y,p)$ for some $y$.
+Deletion requires a live record with its target identifier. The certified
+execution retains these original mint checks and mint honesty. It includes
+ordinary store steps and steps using recursively synthesized virtual merge
+bases; scratch computations do not mint new events.
+
+The sequential specification has tagged lists $(i,x)$ as abstract states.
+Its update labels retain the original event inputs. An insertion at the root
+prepends $(t,x)$; an insertion with a nonzero anchor splices $(t,x)$ immediately
+after the tagged anchor when it is present; if that anchor is absent, the
+abstract insertion leaves the list unchanged. Deletion removes the named
+identifier when present and otherwise leaves the list unchanged. The query
+returns the value list. The abstract update ignores the coordinate used for
+concrete placement.
+
+Admission also checks the original labels. An insertion identifier is greater
+than every earlier insertion identifier. A nonroot insertion names an earlier
+birth with the matching carried coordinate; deletion names an earlier birth.
+The latter condition permits repeated deletion, while excluding fabricated
+identifiers. These history conditions are defined independently of the stored
+implementation state. In particular, they are not obtained by declaring every
+concrete replay to be a legal sequential execution.
+
+\paragraph{The valid-state domain.}
+At a certified configuration $C$, define
+\[
+ I_C(s)\iff s\text{ is sorted}\ \land\
+ \forall z\in s.\ \exists e\in\mathcal E_C.\
+ e\text{ is an insertion and }z=(e.t,e.x,k(e)).
+\]
+Here $e.x$ denotes the value in an insertion payload. The predicate records
+immutable provenance; it does not require the state to be a causally ready
+prefix. Original mint honesty establishes coordinate consistency. The initial
+state satisfies $I_C$, every eligible original event preserves it on any valid
+state, and merging valid states preserves it. Thus the domain includes states
+obtained by reordering or repeating already-issued events.
+
+Use concrete commutation on this domain,
+\[
+ a\mathrel{\comm_{I_C}}b\iff
+ \forall s.\ I_C(s)\Longrightarrow
+ \F{do}(\F{do}(s,a),b)=\F{do}(\F{do}(s,b),a).
+\]
+For supported original events, two insertions commute and two deletions
+commute. An insertion and a deletion commute exactly when the deletion does
+not name that insertion's identifier. Deletion of an insertion's own birth
+therefore remains a genuine conflict. Set the payload relation $\rcrel$ to
+empty and compute the public implementation order with $\comm_{I_C}$ in both
+its causal and absorber clauses. Write this order as $\F{lo}^{C,I_C}_E$.
+It preserves visible noncommuting pairs; it need not preserve all visibility.
+
+\subsection{Concrete Merge and Certified Execution}
+
+The merge argument uses representation indexed by the context and event set.
+Write $R_C(E,s)$ when the context has honest births and targets, visibility is
+transitive and irreflexive, $E\subseteq\mathcal E_C$, and a causal enumeration
+of $E$ folds exactly to $s$. Every metadata dependency is a visibility edge;
+for this instance we retain \emph{all} visibility edges. Reconstruction may
+need an anchor's coordinate even when the corresponding implementation
+operations commute.
+
+The five concrete merge equations hold on represented inputs. The replay
+supply also provides a last event maximal in both the semantic and metadata
+relations, with represented remainder and metadata-past states. Representation
+is unique: two causal enumerations of the same honestly issued history have
+identical concrete folds. These premises instantiate the strict event-set
+induction for representation Join.
+
+To use the generic certified-execution theorem, we additionally connect this
+representation to the implementation's internal event-policy replay witnesses.
+On supported, causally closed histories in a mint-honest context, a stored
+state has such a canonical witness exactly when it is represented. The forward
+direction constructs a causal enumeration and compares folds; the reverse
+direction transports the causal enumeration's order obligations. Consequently
+representation Join supplies the full-causal-closure canonical Join contract.
+The generic store induction then applies, including the nested induction for
+recursive virtual merge bases.
+
+\begin{theorem}[Embedded-RGA execution representation]
+\label{thm:rga-representation}
+Fix an ordered prefix code $\Gamma$ and the implementation and issuer above.
+At every configuration of a finite issuance-certified ordinary or
+recursive-virtual execution from the initial store, every allocated version
+$S(v)=(s,E)$ satisfies $R_C(E,s)$ and $I_C(s)$. For allocated input versions,
+the recursively synthesized virtual merge base represents their intersection
+history and also satisfies $I_C$.
+\end{theorem}
+
+The internal canonical witnesses in this theorem supply exact folds for the
+store induction. The theorem does not identify all linear extensions of the
+public invariant order with a single value. In particular, its fully causal
+Join contract should not be confused with a Join premise over every merely
+conflict-closed set.
