+
+The implementation replay theorem has a different quantifier domain. It proves
+that two legal causal replays of the same represented history have equal
+concrete folds, using the generic invariant replay theorem. The selected
+sequential witness can move causal deletions and is justified instead by its
+explicit reconstruction lemma. Neither argument is a claim that every
+permutation respecting only $\F{lo}^{C,I_C}_E$ has the same fold.
+
+The invariant-order result is also distinct from correctness under raw
+all-state commutation. A certified execution separates the two: an unrelated
+birth and deletion commute on the valid-state domain, but a malformed scratch
+state makes them fail raw commutation and introduces an additional raw order
+edge. That edge excludes every independent explaining history in the checked
+execution. The revised criterion admits its explaining history while retaining
+the real birth/delete conflict. The domain restriction is therefore an explicit
+change to the implementation order, not a proof that the raw criterion already
+held.
